>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Is law a good career in India? Yes, but the honest answer starts by rejecting the question's hidden assumption: most law graduates never argue a single case in court.</strong>
    A law degree is a field-level credential that opens at least six genuinely different branches, litigation,
    corporate and in-house counsel, judiciary, LPO and compliance, policy and legislative work, and academia,
    each with its own entry route, pay range, and daily reality. CLAT 2026 offered roughly 4,092 undergraduate
    seats across 26 National Law Universities for the 5-year integrated route, while a 3-year LLB after any
    bachelor's degree stays open to graduates from every other field. Which branch you actually build toward,
    not the degree itself, decides whether this field moves you toward a real <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong>
    and earlier financial freedom, or toward years of drift inside a "respected" label.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Yes, law is a good career field in India, but treat it as six different branches with very different pay and daily work, not one job.</li>
      <li>The 5-year integrated BA-LLB via CLAT and the 3-year LLB after another degree are both legitimate routes; the right one depends on your age and existing background, not which "sounds more serious."</li>
      <li>Corporate/in-house work currently pays the strongest early-career salaries for graduates who land it; litigation pays the least in the first few years for most graduates.</li>
      <li>Judiciary offers real, government-scale pay, but a 2025 Supreme Court rule now requires three years of legal practice before you can even sit most state judicial service exams.</li>
      <li><strong>The real decision is not "should I study law." It is which specific branch, and which entry route into it, actually fits your interests, your timeline, and your family's budget.</strong></li>
      <li>Test your fit with one real research memo or internship before committing five years and a real fee to a specific college.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    This article is about <a href="/blog/career-options/">law as a field</a>: the degree routes, the branch
    map beyond the courtroom, and the field-level economics of CLAT, college fees, and pay across every
    major branch. If your real question is narrower, specifically the day-to-day life of a practising
    litigator, chamber-junior years, and courtroom work, <a href="/blog/career-options/is-lawyer-a-good-career-in-india/">is lawyer a good career in India</a> covers
    that specific comparison directly, rather than assuming litigation is the only outcome a law degree
    leads to.
  </p>

