  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is law a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    Law remains a genuinely viable, in-demand field in India, and it is far bigger than the courtroom image
    most families carry into the decision. Corporate legal teams, compliance functions, LPOs, policy bodies,
    and law schools all need trained legal graduates, and several of these branches are growing faster than
    litigation seat availability under established seniors.
  </p>

  <p>
    But "good career" does not mean "any LLB, any college, guaranteed courtroom glamour or corporate riches."
  </p>

  <p>
    It means: real demand exists in specific, well-defined branches, real pay exists for graduates who build
    deliberately toward those branches, and a genuinely uneven, sometimes brutal, first few years exist too,
    especially for the litigation path most families default to imagining when they hear "law."
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not the "law is a prestigious, guaranteed-respectable career" story that pulls students toward
      a CLAT seat without a plan. It is also not the cynical "law only pays if you become a top-firm partner
      or a judge" version some families default to. Both miss the real picture: law is a field with several
      legitimate, differently-paced income tracks, and the branch you choose, not the degree by itself,
      decides which of those stories becomes true for you.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Picking a branch late, or by accident, is the single most expensive mistake inside this field. If you
    are already feeling that pressure, comparing branches against your actual budget and timeline with a
    real person, through <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>,
    is usually more useful at this stage than another week of reading forum threads alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-different">Why this question needs a field-level answer, not a courtroom story</h2>

  <p>
    Most "is X a good career" questions compare one job against another. Law is different: the same degree,
    the same three or five years of training, genuinely branches into work that looks nothing alike, in
    daily rhythm, in pay, and in what kind of person actually thrives in it.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">What actually makes law different</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>A litigator, a corporate transactional associate, a compliance officer, a judge, and a law professor all hold the same base credential and do almost entirely different jobs.</li>
      <li>Pay ranges across these branches do not overlap much at entry level: a first-year litigation junior and a first-year Tier-1 firm associate can be earning ten to fifteen times apart.</li>
      <li>Regulation is reshaping the field mid-career for current students, the judiciary's 2025 practice-first rule and the Bar Council's still-developing AI-conduct rules both changed the ground rules in the last two years.</li>
      <li>Unlike medicine or CA, there is no single unifying licensing exam gate at the very start; AIBE and state bar enrolment matter for practice, but several branches, in-house, LPO, policy, academia, do not strictly require them at all.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      Two true things at once: a law degree is one of the most branch-flexible professional credentials in
      India, and picking a branch late or by accident wastes far more of the training than choosing the
      wrong specialisation does in most single-track fields.
    </p>
  </div>

