  <h2 id="clat-economics">CLAT, NLU fees, and the real cost math</h2>

  <p>
    Families often size up "law college cost" against engineering or medicine and conclude it is cheap by
    comparison. At the NLU level that is broadly true; at the private-college level it is not automatically
    true at all, and the fee tells you almost nothing about placement quality on its own.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {clatFactRows.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">Cost fact</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.label}</div>
        <p>{item.detail}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Use roughly 10% of your family's total education budget as a starting benchmark for the course fee
      itself, a strict planning heuristic, not a universal rule. A high private-college fee is only
      justified when it is tied to a verified, course-specific advantage, a genuinely strong placement
      committee, real internship pipelines, faculty with active practice or research, not a glossy campus or
      a brochure full of stock photos.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    One check matters more than the fee itself: confirm the specific college holds current Bar Council of
    India (BCI) approval before paying admission fees, at an NLU or a private college. A law degree from a
    college without valid BCI approval can block enrolment as an advocate later, regardless of how good the
    campus or the marks looked at the time.
  </p>

  <p>
    CLAT coaching is a real, separate cost many families do not budget for upfront, commonly running from a
    few thousand rupees for basic study material to well over a lakh for a multi-year classroom programme.
    Treat it the same way as any other coaching spend: test with free or low-cost mock tests and official
    CLAT preparation material first, and only commit to an expensive programme if a genuine gap in a specific
    section, logical reasoning or comprehension speed, justifies it.
  </p>

