  <h2 id="corporate-in-house">Corporate law firms vs in-house counsel</h2>

  <p>
    These two branches get lumped together as "corporate law," but they are genuinely different careers with
    different pay curves, hours, and what your day-to-day work actually looks like.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tier-1 firm associate life, roughly Rs 15-22.5 LPA at the fresher level at firms like Cyril Amarchand
    Mangaldas, AZB & Partners, Khaitan & Co, Trilegal, Shardul Amarchand Mangaldas, and JSA, comes with
    genuinely long hours during live deals. The highest-paying teams inside these firms in 2026 are mergers
    and acquisitions, private equity, capital markets, competition law, and banking and finance, the
    transactional groups billing the largest deals.
  </p>

  <p>
    In-house counsel roles, spanning roughly Rs 18-80+ LPA across the full seniority range, generally trade
    some of that top-end pay ceiling for meaningfully better hours, closer to 9-10 hour days rather than the
    deal-driven surges firm associates absorb.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The Tier-1 firm number is the one every family repeats when they say "law pays well," and it is real.
      It is also not the modal outcome. These seats go overwhelmingly to graduates of the top-ranked NLUs
      plus a handful of other law schools who clear firm interviews, a genuinely small slice of each year's
      total LLB output. If your target college does not have a strong Tier-1 recruiting pipeline, the honest
      plan is to target in-house roles, mid-size firms, or a specific regulatory niche instead of assuming the
      Tier-1 number applies to you by default.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="lpo-compliance">LPO, compliance, and contract work</h2>

  <p>
    This is the branch families hear about least, and it is genuinely the fastest, lowest-friction entry
    point into paid legal work after graduation.
  </p>

  <p>
    India handles a large share of the world's outsourced legal document work, contract review, e-discovery,
    due-diligence support, IP management, and compliance tracking for domestic and global clients. Entry pay
    commonly runs Rs 25,000-45,000 a month at larger LPOs, rising toward Rs 70,000 a month within roughly two
    years, with senior legal analyst and document-review lead roles reaching Rs 8-18 LPA.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is real, accessible income, and it is also the branch AI-assisted document review is compressing
    fastest. That makes moving from pure volume work toward a specialisation, IP, data-privacy compliance, or
    contract negotiation support, matter more here than in almost any other branch.
  </p>

  <h2 id="policy-academia">Policy, legislative drafting, and academia</h2>

  <p>
    These are the smallest branches by headcount, and also two of the more genuinely growing ones, for
    reasons most career conversations skip entirely.
  </p>

  <p>
    India has been actively rewriting major legal codes in recent years, replacing colonial-era criminal law
    statutes with new frameworks, and expanding sector-specific regulation in data protection, fintech, and
    environmental compliance. That creates real, ongoing demand for people who can read a statute and write
    one, at legislative research bodies, regulatory ministries, and policy think tanks.
  </p>

  <p>
    Pay here starts more modestly than corporate work at most of these bodies, but it builds a distinct kind
    of career capital, policy fluency and legislative drafting skill, that is scarce and valued once you have
    five or more years of it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Academia, usually reached through an LLM plus UGC-NET or a PhD, offers slower income growth than
    corporate work but a defined, stable public-sector pay scale, and a genuinely growing number of law
    schools that need qualified faculty as legal education itself expands.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-risk">What AI is actually changing in law right now</h2>

  <p>
    Two opposite reassurances get repeated to worried law students: "AI could never replace legal judgment"
    and "AI is about to make lawyers obsolete." Look at what actually happened in an Indian courtroom in
    2026, and both turn out to be the wrong framing.
  </p>

  <p>
    In July 2026, the Supreme Court set aside a tribunal ruling that had been built on six fabricated,
    AI-hallucinated precedents, ruling that even a single piece of hallucinated material voids a decision,
    and directed the Bar Council of India to frame disciplinary rules for unverified AI-generated filings.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Bar Council's existing Standards of Professional Conduct and Etiquette do not yet address AI use
    directly, which leaves real, unresolved questions about a lawyer's duty to verify AI output, disclose its
    use to clients, and protect client confidentiality when data is fed into a third-party tool. The Bar
    Council has separately directed law schools to add AI to the curriculum, but teaching AI as a subject and
    training graduates to actually supervise, question, and overrule it are two different things.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What is shrinking</div>
      <ul>
        {aiComparisonLeft.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What is growing</div>
      <ul>
        {aiComparisonRight.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The practical takeaway for someone still choosing this field: AI is making the routine, high-volume parts
    of legal work faster, not making legal judgment unnecessary. There is a genuine risk worth naming
    honestly, too: a graduate who leans on AI-generated research without ever building the verification
    instinct to catch a fabricated citation is building a fast first draft and weak judgment, exactly the gap
    that just cost a real litigant a Supreme Court case. The staged, realistic path is to use AI research and
    drafting tools now to compress routine work, then, as regulation and firm policy mature over the next few
    years, treat verification and override judgment as a core professional skill, not an optional extra.
  </p>

