  <h2 id="ceiling">How to actually raise your income ceiling</h2>

  <p>
    A fair salary table only tells you where most people land, not where the strongest outcomes actually come
    from. This field's income ceiling is not fixed by which college you attended; it is set by how
    deliberately you move from a generic legal credential toward a specific, scarce combination of skills.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      The clearest ceiling-raising moves here are: building a genuine sector specialisation, data protection,
      fintech regulation, IP and patent work, competition law, once basic legal training and one or two
      strong internships exist; moving from a generalist litigation practice toward a specific court or
      subject-matter reputation; moving from a corporate firm associate role toward in-house leadership or
      independent advisory work once a real client network forms; and, for graduates who combine law with
      another degree, engineering plus IP, commerce plus tax, building a niche few pure-law graduates can
      match.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of these are guaranteed outcomes, and none happen from the degree alone. They happen for graduates
      who pair real training with visible proof, a strong writing sample, a specific internship record, or a
      published research note, and enough professional discipline to build a reputation deliberately, not
      just accumulate years in a single generalist role.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    An LLM, whether from an Indian NLU or a foreign university, is one specific way to buy that
    specialisation faster, but it works best layered on top of real internship or work experience aimed at a
    named practice area, not as a generic "add a masters" move for someone still unsure which branch fits.
  </p>

  <p>
    On AI specifically, the realistic path is staged, not a single leap. Right now, the useful move is
    learning to work alongside AI-assisted research and drafting tools so routine document work takes a
    fraction of the time it used to. As Bar Council rules and firm policies mature over the next few years,
    the bigger opportunity shifts toward owning the verification and judgment layer, catching a fabricated
    citation, spotting a risk an AI tool missed, and using the freed-up time for the specialisation and
    client-facing work that AI genuinely cannot replace.
  </p>

