  <h2 id="parents">What to tell a worried family</h2>

  <p>
    A worried parent rarely calms down because someone tells them "don't worry." They calm down when the
    actual branches, numbers, and timeline are on the table, especially once they realise "law" does not
    automatically mean "courtroom."
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What worries most families</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Fear that "law" only means courtroom work, with the well-known uncertainty of early litigation income.</li>
        <li>Stories about junior lawyers struggling financially in the first few years.</li>
        <li>Not knowing whether an NLU seat, a private college, or a 3-year route after another degree matters most for a specific target branch.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What actually reassures them</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Six genuinely different branches exist, litigation, corporate/in-house, judiciary, LPO/compliance, policy, and academia, each with its own income timeline, not one uncertain path.</li>
        <li>A realistic income timeline: a modest or uneven first year or two in some branches, meaningfully steadier growth by year three to five once a branch and specialisation are chosen, with in-house, judiciary, and corporate tracks offering defined, verifiable pay bands well before that.</li>
        <li>One visible proof step already taken, like a completed internship, a real research memo, or a shortlisted CLAT or bar-exam preparation plan, not just an intention to "study law."</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Another week of turning "is law a good career" over in your head, in the abstract, will not settle
    anything. Neither will letting a relative's opinion about "a respectable, safe profession" make the call
    on your behalf.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Run yourself through The 4-Checkpoint Protocol above, honestly, on paper, for the actual branch and
      college you are considering.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then pass The 3 Gates on one real research memo or a genuine internship application before you commit
      CLAT preparation time, course fees, and five years to a specific route.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Achieving earlier financial freedom through a career in law comes down to building a genuine
    <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> inside a deliberately chosen branch, real research and
    drafting proof, a specific internship record, and the ability to explain and defend your decisions
    clearly, not the word "law graduate" on a résumé alone. Move toward that with
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>
    if you want a second opinion on your specific situation, or start with the free
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career and skill assessments</a>
    if you are still unsure whether law, or which branch of it, is genuinely your fit. For a wider comparison
